The five bitwise and logical operators each get their own precedence.

WHAT IT IS. One function tried all five -- & && | || ^ -- at a SINGLE level and
recursed into itself, so they parsed flat, left to right:

    1 | 2 ^ 3      read as (1|2)^3 = 0        C says 1|(2^3) = 1
    6 ^ 3 & 1      read as (6^3)&1 = 1        C says 6^(3&1) = 7
    1 || 0 && 0    read as (1||0)&&0 = 0      C says 1||(0&&0) = 1

AND THE NOTE IN MICRO-C.md WAS WRONG IN TWO WAYS, which is worth correcting
rather than quietly fixing. It recorded this as `constant_expression`
"folding right-to-left".

  - It is not confined to the constant parser. Every one of the three lines
    above is an ordinary runtime expression.
  - It is not right-to-left. It is left-to-right with NO PRECEDENCE AT ALL,
    which is a different bug and would have been diagnosed differently: a
    right-to-left fold gets `1|2^3` RIGHT by accident.

The note had never been checked. What settled it was three lines of C and their
exit codes -- the cheapest measurement in this repository, and the one this
file keeps recording as the thing that should have been done first.

THE FIX. C's order, tightest first, is  &  ^  |  &&  ||  and all five are looser
than == and !=, which relational_expr already handles. So the chain sits on top
of it exactly as C's grammar does:

    bitwise_and_expr  : relational_expr    ( '&'  relational_expr    )*
    bitwise_xor_expr  : bitwise_and_expr   ( '^'  bitwise_and_expr   )*
    bitwise_or_expr   : bitwise_xor_expr   ( '|'  bitwise_xor_expr   )*
    logical_and_expr  : bitwise_or_expr    ( '&&' bitwise_or_expr    )*
    bitwise_expr      : logical_and_expr   ( '||' logical_and_expr   )*

The per-architecture instruction strings move into three small functions so
each tier names one operator instead of one function naming five across six
architectures. `match` is an exact string compare and the tokeniser emits `&&`
as one token, so `&` cannot swallow it.

ARITHMETIC PRECEDENCE WAS ALWAYS RIGHT -- `1+2*3` is 7 -- because
additive_expr_a/b/c is already a tiered chain. This is the same shape for the
tier nobody had written, which is also why it survived: the obvious probe
passes.

WHY tcc NEVER SHOWED IT. Scanning tcc for expressions that mix these tiers
without parentheses at the same depth finds essentially nothing: tcc
parenthesises `x & (A|B)` everywhere, and the three logical-tier candidates are
a string literal and two comments. No amount of staring at tcc would have found
this. Rows 193, 196 and 197 of stage 2's conformance corpus did, in a second.

MEASURED

    difftest      56 -> 57 pass, both architectures, nothing regressed
    vocabulary    clean on five architectures -- all six instruction tables
                  moved, so this is the gate that matters here
    corpus        419 -> 422 of 426 on aarch64
    tcc           compiles, links and runs as before; the fault has NOT moved

